(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-glaucoma-diagnostics-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Glaucoma & Diagnostic Agents Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A patient has had proparacaine for foreign body removal. What must they be told, and what must you NOT do?","choices":["The eye stays numb for several hours, and a short prescription is reasonable for pain","Vision will blur for about twenty minutes, and they should not drive","They should keep the eye open to let the drug disperse","Numb for 10 to 20 minutes, no blink reflex, never prescribe"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. No blink reflex means the eye is unprotected for the duration. And these are explicitly not to be prescribed for home use — repeated application is toxic to the epithelium and delays healing. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which patients with raised pressure are treated, and which are monitored?","choices":["Treat those with risk factors; monitor those without","All patients with pressure above 21 mmHg are treated","Only those who already have visual field loss are treated","Treatment is started only once both eyes are affected"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Raised pressure alone is not automatically a prescription. Risk factors decide, and in their absence you watch for the change that would make it a disease. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which classes reduce aqueous PRODUCTION?","choices":["Prostaglandins, beta blockers and cholinergic agonists","Cholinergic agonists and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors only","Prostaglandins alone","Alpha agonists, beta blockers and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The alpha agonists sit on both sides of the list, which is the part most easily got wrong — they reduce production AND increase outflow. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What does fluorescein show, and what is it used for?","choices":["Raised intraocular pressure, for glaucoma screening","Fungal elements, for suspected fungal keratitis","Epithelial defects of cornea and conjunctiva","Retinal detachment, by staining the posterior segment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It reveals where the epithelium is broken. Its adverse effects are hypersensitivity and a burning sensation, which is true of most things put on an injured eye. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Which adverse effects most affect whether a patient will keep taking a topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitor?","choices":["Bitter taste and stinging","Bradycardia and heart block","Iris darkening and eyelash growth","Fixed small pupils and myopia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Neither is dangerous, but between them they affect over half of patients — and a drop that tastes foul and stings is a drop that gets skipped. The taste comes from nasolacrimal drainage to the pharynx. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"What is the general target for pressure reduction in glaucoma therapy?","choices":["A 20 to 30 percent reduction in intraocular pressure","A pressure below 10 mmHg in every patient","A 50 percent reduction regardless of starting pressure","Any reduction at all, however small"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The goal is expressed as a proportion rather than an absolute number, which matches the stated aim: prevent progressive optic nerve damage with the least risk and fewest side effects. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"For which procedures are topical ocular anesthetics indicated?","choices":["Fundoscopy and pupil assessment","Tonometry, foreign body removal, corneal surgery","Treatment of corneal abrasion pain at home","Intravitreal injection only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. All three need the surface insensate but not the eye immobilized. That also explains why tonometry cannot simply be done on any patient without preparation. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Besides fundoscopy, what therapeutic use do cycloplegics have?","choices":["Glaucoma, to increase aqueous outflow","Uveitis, to prevent synechiae and relieve ciliary spasm","Bacterial conjunctivitis, to reduce photophobia","Dry eye, to reduce tear evaporation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Keeping the pupil moving stops the iris adhering, and relaxing the ciliary muscle relieves the pain of spasm. So they are not purely diagnostic agents. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Why are younger patients usually intolerant of miotic therapy?","choices":["Systemic bradycardia from muscarinic activation","Permanent iris color change","Visual blurring from induced myopia","Increased airway resistance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Fixed small pupils, induced myopia and visual disturbance are the effects, and a young eye with active accommodation notices them most. That, plus frequent dosing, is why compliance is poor. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What should a patient be warned of after a cycloplegic agent is instilled?","choices":["A bitter taste and stinging","Photosensitivity and blurred vision","Fixed, small pupils and myopia","Conjunctival hyperemia with lash growth"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both follow directly from a dilated pupil and a paralyzed ciliary muscle, so they are predictable rather than idiosyncratic. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which classes INCREASE aqueous outflow?","choices":["Beta blockers, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors and prostaglandins","Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors and cholinergic agonists only","Beta blockers alone","Prostaglandins, alpha adrenergic agonists and cholinergic agonists"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The alpha agonists appear on both sides — they decrease production and increase outflow — which is why they are easy to misplace. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What distinguishes angle closure glaucoma from open angle glaucoma?","choices":["Increased aqueous production with a gradual course","Optic nerve damage at a normal intraocular pressure","Blockage of the drainage canal, with acute pain","Raised pressure without any optic nerve damage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Open angle comes from increased production or reduced drainage and damages the nerve over time; angle closure is a mechanical blockage that presents acutely and painfully. Drug therapy targets open angle disease. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"How do dorzolamide and brinzolamide lower pressure?","choices":["They increase outflow through the trabecular meshwork","They block beta receptors in the ciliary body","They activate muscarinic receptors on the ciliary muscle","They inhibit carbonic anhydrase in the ciliary epithelium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Less bicarbonate means less fluid follows it, so aqueous production falls. It is a production-side drug, like the beta blockers but by a different route. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Which classes are named as reasonable places to START glaucoma therapy?","choices":["A prostaglandin analog or a beta blocker","A carbonic anhydrase inhibitor or a cholinergic agonist","A combination product from the outset","A cholinergic agonist alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Either is a reasonable opening move, though the prostaglandins are described as first line and the most commonly used, with beta blockers second line. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Phenylephrine also dilates the pupil. How does it differ from the antimuscarinics?","choices":["It is an antimuscarinic with a longer duration","It produces cycloplegia without mydriasis","It is a sympathomimetic, more light-reactive","It constricts rather than dilates"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Two routes to mydriasis — block the constrictor or stimulate the dilator — and the sympathomimetic leaves more light reactivity, which matters if the pupil response still needs assessing. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Why might glaucoma therapy be started in one eye only?","choices":["To judge efficacy and tolerability against the untreated eye","Because bilateral treatment doubles the systemic absorption to unsafe levels","Because only the worse eye requires treatment","To reduce the cost of therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The other eye acts as its own control, which tells you whether the drug is doing anything and whether the patient can tolerate it before both eyes are committed. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"How do tetracaine and proparacaine work?","choices":["They block muscarinic receptors on the ciliary muscle","They inhibit sodium influx into the neuron","They activate alpha-1 receptors to vasoconstrict","They inhibit prostaglandin synthesis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Blocking the sodium channel stops the action potential, so the nerve cannot signal. It is the same mechanism as any local anesthetic, applied to the ocular surface. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 75"}]};
